(PB 20.9.1) The <s>Bahiṣpavamāna</s> is threefold; the <s>Ājya</s> of the <s>Hotṛ</s> is fifteenfold; of the <s>Maitrāvaruṇa</s> seventeenfold; of the <s>Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin</s> twenty-onefold; of the <s>Acchāvāka</s> fifteenfold; the midday <s>Pavamāna</s> is seventeenfold; the <s>Pṛṣṭha</s> of the <s>Hotṛ</s> is twenty-onefold; of the <s>Maitrāvaruṇa</s> twenty-sevenfold;
(PB 20.9.1) of the <s>Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin</s> seventeenfold; of the <s>Acchāvāka</s> twenty-onefold; the <s>Ārbhava</s> is twenty-sevenfold; the <s>Agniṣṭoma</s> is thirty-threefold; the <s>Uktha</s> chants descend — twenty-seven first, then two twenty-one with <s>Ṣoḍaśin</s>; the night is fifteenfold; the <s>Sandhi</s> is threefold. One desiring cattle should sacrifice with a <s>Viśvajit Atirātra</s>.
(PB 20.9.2) Seed is the <s>Nābhānediṣṭhīya</s>; cattle are the <s>Vālakhilya</s>. Since the <s>Nābhānediṣṭhīya</s> is recited first and the <s>Vālakhilya</s> after — from the seed cattle are born. He diversifies the forms through the <s>Vārṣākapa</s>; he becomes established in the seasons through the <s>Evayāmarut</s>.
